In Java, the `==` operator checks for reference equality, i.e., whether two variables refer to the same object in memory. In contrast, the `.equals()` method is a commonly used method for comparing the content or value of two objects, including strings.

When comparing strings in Java, you should use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. This is because two string variables may refer to the same object in memory (i.e., have the same reference), even if their values are different. For example:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";

// These two strings have the same value, but different references
System.out.println(str1 == str2); // false

// Use the .equals() method to compare their values
System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // true
```

The `==` operator should only be used to compare primitive types (e.g., `int`, `char`, `boolean`) or when comparing references to objects for identity (e.g., checking if a variable is `null`). 

In summary, when comparing strings in Java, always use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. This ensures that you are comparing the actual values of the strings, rather than just their references in memory.

